this week congress took a major step by  allowing some of its own staffers to  unionize  so far the decision would apply only to  the house not the senate it comes amid a  reinvigorated movement over labor rights  as workers take on some of the biggest  corporations in the country  here's congressional correspondent  nicole killian  for a decade working on capitol hill  seemed like a dream job for rich lucetti  every day you come to work in the  capitol you're working in a living  museum the same time the first job that  i worked in i remember i  made thirty three thousand dollars a  year i was splitting an apartment with a  college friend  it wasn't uncommon to get a meal at  seven eleven he finally reached a  breaking point working long hours  working through covid and then on top of  that the january 6th insurrection it  just got to a point where  i enjoyed my 10 years here but it was  time for me to go last year turnover  among house staffers reached its highest  rate in at least 20 years a recent  report found roughly one in eight  congressional staffers make less than a  living wage  after a viral outcry for months over low  pay lack of diversity and hostile work  environments on the anonymous instagram  account dear white staffers the  resolution  is adopted  the house approved a resolution this  week allowing nearly 10 000  congressional workers the right to  organize and collectively bargain it's a  really big deal just to say as a matter  of law  you  staffers of capitol hill  have the right to form a union michigan  congressman and 11 introduced the  measure why do you think it's gaining  traction now  you know i think that this is part of a  much broader movement in society  so starting with teachers then you had  workers in industry after industry start  to form unions  amazon now we have starbucks the coffee  giant recently announced new pay hikes  and benefits for employees but excluded  those who have formed unions at its  stores  this spring amazon workers established  the first union in the online retailer's  nearly 28-year history at a warehouse in  staten island you got to treat our  people right drawing attention and  scrutiny from lawmakers you've  determined amazon is a piece of crap  company  that's your political bias it's not a  democrat or republican thing  it's a workers thing for rich the action  by congress is an important first step  if you're asking do i wish i had been a  union member 10 years ago when i was  making 33 000 a year absolutely people  are working longer and longer hours  right now for less money on top of that  inflation is just running away and  there's a recognition that people who  work hard should be able to provide for  themselves and for their country for cbs  saturday morning nicole killian  washington
 

